{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/control-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-eligibility/controls/control-packet-7.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage B: reading the control theses (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief\nand the one packet you are given.\n\nEach item of your packet pairs a **ruler question** (a question written by hand for an earlier, frozen measurement, with its\nunit, due day and ladder of rungs) with a **judged question** the system wrote itself for a thesis it opened (its question, the\noutcome it looks at, due day, year and rungs). It also lists **reader B's decisive items**: the evidence an earlier reader, called\nreader B, cited for the frozen answer to the ruler question (each with its day, title, passage and meaning), and **the evidence\nshown to the judge**: every evidence entry the system's judge was shown for the judged question in any of its requests on or\nbefore 2026-07-14 (empty when there is none). You are not told either answer, and you do not judge which answer is right.\n\nFor each item, answer three questions.\n\n1. `same_question`: **yes** when the judged question asks what the ruler question asks: the same quantity measured the same way\n   (the same total, scope and unit: not a part of it, a ceiling on it, another quantity or another unit), or the same event,\n   for the same period. **no** otherwise. Wording may differ; a judged question that asks for more or less than the ruler's,\n   or for another period, is **no**.\n2. `same_due`: for an **event** (a yes-or-no question), **yes** only when the judged question's deadline is the ruler's\n   deadline. For a **figure**, **yes** when the two due days both mark the publication of the same period's figure (for\n   example, both the annual report of the same fiscal year, even if the two days differ); **no** otherwise.\n3. `items`: for **each** of reader B's decisive items, by its id, **shown** when its content is present in the evidence shown to\n   the judge (the same fact or figure, in any wording, from any source), **not_shown** when it is absent. Search the entries for\n   the figure or the fact; do not mark an item shown because a related item is.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"control\": 3, \"same_question\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"same_due\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"items\": {\"i005\": \"shown\"|\"not_shown\", ...}, \"why\": \"one sentence naming what decides each no and each not_shown\"}`.\n\n## Third reader (only where the two readers differ)\n\nA third reader of each family is given this brief, the packet, and a list of the disputed points, each a control number and a\nfield (`same_question`, `same_due`, or `item <id>`), without the two readers' answers. For each point, answer that one question\nafresh by the rules above and return one JSON object per point, one per line, and nothing else:\n`{\"control\": 3, \"field\": \"item i005\", \"ruling\": \"yes\"|\"no\"|\"shown\"|\"not_shown\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}` (`yes`/`no` for the two\nquestion fields, `shown`/`not_shown` for an item).\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 25,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion to below $900 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 900000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$900 billion to below $1000 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 900000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 1000000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$1000 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 1000000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n   \"question\": \"What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-03-30\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 1000000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\")\",\n    \"lo\": 1000000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 1200000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 1200000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 1500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"lo\": 1500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i090\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n    \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast\",\n     \"how_much\": \"82% this year; 41% next year\",\n     \"period\": \"this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i143\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"passage\": \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\",\n     \"what\": \"memory's share of data center spend\",\n     \"how_much\": \"about half\",\n     \"period\": \"2027\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i161\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\",\n    \"passage\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs estimate\",\n     \"what\": \"annual AI infrastructure capex (chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking)\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$765B (2026); $1.6T (2031); ~$7.6T cumulative 2026-2031\",\n     \"period\": \"2026; 2031; 2026-2031\",\n     \"condition\": \"could reach\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e125 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] On its most recent quarterly conference call, NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e55 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that public opposition to data centers — blamed for large increases in power/utility costs and water consumption, with 7 in 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026, a demand signal for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. … These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e114 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\\n    Quote: Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\",\n   \"- e147 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, and that 100 gigawatts are expected to be online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e161 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware, and polling cited in the same reporting found 7 in 10 Americans oppose a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e55; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e197 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article reports that Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027).\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e205 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source AI hyperscalers (capex plans; 2027 forecast is the author's) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e250 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by Futuriom); original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported (July 1) that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models, on the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model; the article notes Meta had planned to buy 'millions' of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year and questions what its move implies for AI demand.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\",\n   \"- e256 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings, a data-center electrical and technology systems contractor, reported March-quarter 2026 results: Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which it attributed to continued strong demand in the data center market; Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; and total backlog rose 62% since the end of fiscal 2025 to $3.86 billion, driven by customer demand and expansion capacity.\\n    Quote: Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million, and total backlog exploded to $3.86 \",\n   \"- e257 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services, a grid and electrical infrastructure contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus, and backlog that rose to a record $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $34.70–35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55–14.25 in adjusted EPS, and cited a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS.\",\n   \"- e258 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA, a mechanical/HVAC contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year (51% organic), diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus, and backlog of $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue\",\n   \"- e259 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA (CFO Bill George) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] On the Q1 2026 call, Comfort Systems USA finance chief Bill George said the constraint on the business is now a supply (labor) issue rather than demand, saying 'There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it' — indicating data-center work demand exceeds the contractor's capacity to execute.\\n    Quote: Today, I think it’s really important for people to understand that it’s a supply issue. There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it.\",\n   \"- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus, Remaining Performance Obligations at a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50–19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25–29.75.\\n    Quote: Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75.\",\n   \"- e261 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 (beating consensus by a double-digit percentage), revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin; it raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\",\n   \"- e395 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (2027).\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\",\n   \"- e404 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source Nvidia (reporting AWS's plan) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\",\n   \"- e469 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley, cited in a 247wallst.com article, forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure 'next year' (calendar 2027), adding about 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, up from roughly 6.7 GW added in 2025; Google alone is expected to add 6.8 GW.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 26,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v2\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA make revenue-generating first shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026, as confirmed by an official NVIDIA shipment announcement or company filing?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether Vera Rubin begins shipping in 2026, as the item reports it 'could begin shipping later this year'.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA makes revenue-generating first shipments of its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026, confirmed by an official NVIDIA shipment announcement or company filing.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After July 2, 2026, NVIDIA formally announces that it has abandoned Vera Rubin or postponed its first shipments until after 2026. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e40 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per the article.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e75 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in March that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time (March 2026) and the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e94 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n   \"- e98 [fact; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU in fall 2026 as part of its Vera Rubin platform, per the article — its first move into the CPU market. The item reports a launch plan/date, not shipments or revenue.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"), per the article, as it prepares to launch its first stand-alone CPU in fall 2026. This is company guidance, not reported revenue.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\",\n   \"- e94 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e121, e179; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, according to the bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale. The bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. … The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n   \"- e121 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley says first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e138 [rumour; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e173 [party_statement; Investing.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted the reported product-roadmap delays, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e170 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, leaving NVIDIA without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now — a gap that could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\",\n   \"- e179 [fact; tikr.com (TIKR); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e188 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Tom's Hardware (Luke James) ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack, and that NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n   \"- e40 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026), per the article.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e242; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e242 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] NVIDIA is working to release its more powerful Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of calendar 2026, the article reports.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75, e308; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e302 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] NVIDIA denied reports that Kyber could be delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. It said Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key part of its long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e256 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the item says this could push NVIDIA to lean further on its new credit and revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\",\n   \"- e334 [party_statement; moomoo; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] NVIDIA issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"), per the article, as it prepares to launch its first stand-alone CPU in fall 2026. This is company guidance, not reported revenue.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e411 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] At NVIDIA's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed for AI workloads and agentic AI systems.\\n    Quote: During the company's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems.\",\n   \"- e412 [rumour; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Reports suggest NVIDIA has begun offering its new Vera processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\",\n   \"- e425 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The article states that NVIDIA recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform (alongside releasing the RTX Spark AI PC platform); it gives no launch date, shipment timing or volume.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\",\n   \"- e426 [rumour; en.sedaily.com (Lee Seok-jin), citing unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; original source LG Electronics 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute-tray specifications of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform in the first half of 2026; LG plans to secure orders from global big-tech customers after reliability evaluations in the second half and to begin mass production of the AI server rack next year (2027). The article attributes the account to unnamed industry sources.\\n    Quote: LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year.\",\n   \"- e542 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping to target shipping dates fast, and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 27,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$550 billion to below $600 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $550 billion and less than $600 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n   \"question\": \"How much did Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet and Meta report spending on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 earnings releases?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"The combined 2026 data-center capex the four largest AI hyperscalers actually report/spend, versus the ~$650 billion currently planned and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for the following year.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"below 500,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\")\",\n    \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"condition\": \"from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD\",\n    \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"800,000,000,000 USD or more\",\n    \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i232\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Alphabet / Google\",\n     \"what\": \"capital expenditure guidance\",\n     \"how_much\": \"up to $190bn\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i233\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\",\n    \"passage\": \"Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Google Cloud\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth and backlog\",\n     \"how_much\": \"revenue +63%; backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i295\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Here are Friday's biggest analyst calls: Nvidia, SpaceX, Toll, Shopify, Twilio, Seagate, Chipotle & more\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company describing \\\"accelerating growth rates\\\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA management, relayed by Morgan Stanley\",\n     \"what\": \"revenue growth rates accelerating\",\n     \"how_much\": \"quarterly revenue approaching $100 bn\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e84 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source The big four AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\",\n   \"- e85 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), as an extension of the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend this year.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\",\n   \"- e54 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Jowi Morales); original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] The article reports that public opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\",\n   \"- e63 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] The item's market-wrap teaser reports that Meta has a US$145 billion AI spending plan, which it says puts chip stocks in focus. This is a hyperscaler customer spending figure bearing on demand for AI chips; no open thesis covers overall hyperscaler AI capex demand.\\n    Quote: Weekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\",\n   \"- e85 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), as an extension of the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend this year.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, according to the bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale. The bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. … The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n   \"- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rises from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\\n    Quote: The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. … As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent o\",\n   \"- e130 [party_statement; Keithen Drury / The Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] During its most recent quarterly conference call, NVIDIA management forecast that AI hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e150 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now costs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e153 [third_party_view; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 24/7 Wall St.; original source 24/7 Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] 24/7 Wall St. estimates that about half of NVIDIA's $75.25 billion in Q1 FY27 data-center revenue came from hyperscalers — the same companies (Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Meta named; later the article says 'six companies') that fund their own custom AI chips. This is the outlet's own analysis, not a company disclosure quoted in the item.\\n    Quote: About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA. … Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list\",\n   \"- e161 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited in the Berkshire Edge column by Allen Harris; that spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking. The item does not say when Goldman published the estimate.\\n    Quote: The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\",\n   \"- e84 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source The big four AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e205, e216; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e85 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), as an extension of the roughly $650 billion they plan to spend this year.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e206; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e205 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Big four AI hyperscalers (Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta) ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the article, which frames this as the current-year aggregate AI infrastructure capex plan of NVIDIA's largest customers.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e206 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] NVIDIA claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year, i.e. calendar 2027, according to the article; this is NVIDIA's forward claim about its largest customers' spending, not an observed outlay.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e216 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (Fool.com); original source Keithen Drury / Fool.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com reported that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year the figure could be over $1 trillion, which the article says will raise demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e222 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63%, with its backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\",\n   \"- e223 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google guided its 2026 capital expenditures up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\",\n   \"- e254 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by futuriom.com); original source Meta Platforms 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1, 2026, that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. The item notes Meta had planned earlier in the year to buy 'millions' of NVIDIA GPUs and questions whether Meta now needs those chips, framing it as a signal about AI demand and possible overbuilding by hyperscalers.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\",\n   \"- e260 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Quanta Services (CEO Earl 'Duke' Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Earl 'Duke' Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta Services, put a figure on the AI-infrastructure opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 arising from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\",\n   \"- e261 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported a 'blowout' Q1 2026: revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus, and backlog that rocketed to a record $48.5 billion.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion.\",\n   \"- e262 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source Comfort Systems USA (CFO Bill George) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA said data-center and technology infrastructure is now about 45% of company revenue and that demand exceeds supply; Q1 2026 revenue was $2.87 billion (up 56.5%, organic +51%), diluted EPS $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier. CFO Bill George said the constraint is a supply issue, with 'plenty more work we could take.'\\n    Quote: Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue, and management says demand still exceeds supply. … The numbers back him up. Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a \",\n   \"- e263 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group's U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while Mechanical Construction added 28.8%, and its Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year.\\n    Quote: Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while Mechanical Construction added 28.8%. … Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year,\",\n   \"- e264 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 247wallst.com; original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported Communications revenue up 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which it attributed to 'continued strong demand in the data center market,' and total backlog jumped to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025 on customer demand and expansion capacity.\\n    Quote: Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” … total backlog exploded to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, fueled by customer demand and expansion capacity.\",\n   \"- e276 [third_party_view; Vivek Arya, BofA Global Research, reported by Investing.com via Yahoo Finance UK; original source BofA Global Research (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] BofA analyst Vivek Arya argued that custom ASIC competition from Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA has not displaced NVIDIA, noting those chips have all existed for years while NVIDIA's GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015; he added that NVIDIA's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Quote: On ASIC competition, Arya pointed out that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have all existed for years, \\\"yet NVDA GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015.\\\" Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e290; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e280 [fact; qz.com (Quartz); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ee0d6792b6bf98069cfe0cc6 2026-07-08] Nvidia reported total revenue of $81.6 billion for its fiscal first quarter of 2027, an 85% increase from a year earlier, and its data center segment set a record at $75.2 billion, according to the article, which cited these results while contrasting Nvidia's growth with Apple's.\\n    Quote: For its fiscal first quarter of 2027, Nvidia posted $81.6 billion in total revenue — an 85% increase from a year earlier — while its data center segment set a record at $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e290 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA's report says NVIDIA's hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, which BofA reads as wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia’s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e276; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e308 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management has articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e332 [fact; moomoo (data compiled by Bloomberg); original source Quanta Computer ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Quanta Computer, an NVIDIA AI-server assembly partner, saw its June 2026 monthly sales more than double year on year to NT$385.19 billion, according to Bloomberg-compiled data, which the article reads as continued strong AI-server demand.\\n    Quote: Still, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\",\n   \"- e333 [fact; moomoo (data compiled by Bloomberg); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Hon Hai Precision Industry (Foxconn), NVIDIA's server assembly partner, posted a 52% year-on-year surge in June 2026 monthly sales to NT$821.8 billion and a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\n    Quote: $HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\",\n   \"- e372 [fact; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, including Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Quote: On the demand side, NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e391 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\",\n   \"- e408 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results: revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion and beat analyst expectations; Data Center revenue rose 92% year over year to $75.2 billion; net income rose 211% to $58.3 billion; and EPS was $2.39.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's latest quarterly results show that AI demand remains exceptionally strong. For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations. Data Center revenue climbed 92% YOY to $75.2 billion, highlighting that AI infrastructure remains the company's primary growth engine. … The company also exceeded earnings estimates. \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e309; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e409 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion while assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\\n    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions. That outlook suggests AI spending remains healthy even without contributions from one of the world's largest technology markets.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e63 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] The item's market-wrap teaser reports that Meta has a US$145 billion AI spending plan, which it says puts chip stocks in focus. This is a hyperscaler customer spending figure bearing on demand for AI chips; no open thesis covers overall hyperscaler AI capex demand.\\n    Quote: Weekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e463; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e254 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by futuriom.com); original source Meta Platforms 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1, 2026, that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. The item notes Meta had planned earlier in the year to buy 'millions' of NVIDIA GPUs and questions whether Meta now needs those chips, framing it as a signal about AI demand and possible overbuilding by hyperscalers.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e464; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e440 [fact; Danny Vena, The Motley Fool (fool.com); original source Amazon ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] Amazon reported Q1 revenue of $182 billion, up 17% year over year, EPS of $2.78, up 75%, and AWS revenue growth of 28%, which the article cites as a reacceleration of its cloud growth.\\n    Quote: In Q1, revenue of $182 billion rose 17% year over year, while EPS of $2.78 jumped 75%. Perhaps more telling is the reacceleration of its cloud growth, as AWS revenue rose 28%.\",\n   \"- e441 [fact; Danny Vena, The Motley Fool (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] Microsoft reported fiscal 2026 third-quarter (ended March 31) revenue of $83 billion, up 18% year over year, diluted EPS of $4.27, up 23%, and Azure cloud revenue growth of 40%.\\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2026 third quarter (ended March 31), Microsoft generated revenue that climbed 18% year over year to $83 billion, while its diluted EPS of $4.27 grew 23%. Perhaps more importantly, its Azure Cloud revenue jumped 40%.\",\n   \"- e444 [fact; Alex Sirois (247wallst.com); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9% year-over-year, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; CEO Hock Tan said the mix was 'driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking,' and Broadcom's adjusted EBITDA margin was 69%.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%. CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.” … Broadcom is compounding a narrow, high-margin ASIC business with an adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e455; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e445 [party_statement; Alex Sirois (247wallst.com); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom guided to $16.0 billion in AI revenue for its Q3 FY2026 (at 200%+ growth per the article's summary), roughly 54% of total sales, ahead of its September 8, 2026 earnings report.\\n    Quote: AVGO's AI revenue surged 143% with a $16B guide at 200%+ growth … Broadcom’s Q3 earnings report lands September 8, and management has already telegraphed $16.0 billion in AI revenue, roughly 54% of total sales.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e140; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e448 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja / 247wallst.com; original source Advanced Micro Devices (Lisa Su) ; from gnews:301b0738dab6ca32e88b019a 2026-07-13] AMD CEO Lisa Su said AMD's MI450 customer forecasts are \\\"exceeding our initial expectations\\\" and that the 6 GW Meta Instinct deployment is now the anchor, per the article — a competitor's forward demand claim and a large customer's deployment of rival AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Lisa Su noted MI450 customer forecasts are “exceeding our initial expectations,” and the 6 GW Meta Instinct deployment is now the anchor.\",\n   \"- e463 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure budget to $125 billion to $145 billion, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e466 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey, 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity, versus roughly 6.7 GW added in 2025; Google alone is forecast to add 6.8 GW, which the article says exceeds what the hyperscalers added combined two years earlier. Reported by 247wallst.com's Rich Duprey from Morgan Stanley.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\",\n   \"- e63 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] The item's market-wrap teaser reports that Meta has a US$145 billion AI spending plan, which it says puts chip stocks in focus. This is a hyperscaler customer spending figure bearing on demand for AI chips; no open thesis covers overall hyperscaler AI capex demand.\\n    Quote: Weekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e463, e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e444 [fact; Alex Sirois (247wallst.com); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9% year-over-year, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; CEO Hock Tan said the mix was 'driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking,' and Broadcom's adjusted EBITDA margin was 69%.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%. CEO Hock Tan said the mix is being “driven by increasing demand for custom AI accelerators and AI networking.” … Broadcom is compounding a narrow, high-margin ASIC business with an adjusted EBITDA margin of 69%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e455, e517; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e448 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja / 247wallst.com; original source Advanced Micro Devices (Lisa Su) ; from gnews:301b0738dab6ca32e88b019a 2026-07-13] AMD CEO Lisa Su said AMD's MI450 customer forecasts are \\\"exceeding our initial expectations\\\" and that the 6 GW Meta Instinct deployment is now the anchor, per the article — a competitor's forward demand claim and a large customer's deployment of rival AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Lisa Su noted MI450 customer forecasts are “exceeding our initial expectations,” and the 6 GW Meta Instinct deployment is now the anchor.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e518; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e471 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The Motley Fool's Justin Pope writes that the four largest AI hyperscalers — Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon — are planning higher capital expenditures in calendar 2026, without giving an amount. The statement bears on the aggregate 2026 data-center capex that drives NVIDIA's demand but adds no new figure.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers, including Meta Platforms , Microsoft , Alphabet , and Amazon , are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026.\",\n   \"- e472 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 (the year of publication) to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as cited by the Motley Fool.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\",\n   \"- e498 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Meta ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta has a $145 billion infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon, cited among competitive threats to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e543 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source Amazon ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon recently borrowed another $25 billion, triggering a minor selloff in AI-related debt as investors worry that big hyperscalers could slow their purchases of Nvidia hardware if returns on AI capital spending disappoint.\\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt . Investors are increasingly worried that if these giant tech firms begin to slow down their hardware acquisition cycles, Nvidia's revenue growth could hit a wall.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 28,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA make first shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack with Vera Rubin Ultra during calendar 2027, as determined by NVIDIA shipment confirmations, earnings-call disclosures, or independently confirmed customer deployments?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether the Kyber rack debuts and ships with Vera Rubin Ultra in calendar 2027 (consistent with NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact') or is delayed to 2028 (SemiAnalysis), and when first shipments actually occur.\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA makes first shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack with Vera Rubin Ultra by 2027-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"condition\": \"After 2026-07-05, NVIDIA announces that it has canceled Kyber or formally deferred its first shipments with Vera Rubin Ultra until 2028. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i179\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n     \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i181\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n     \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\",\n     \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i101\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i197\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n     \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n     \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post published Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which is designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its next-generation Kyber rack system is delayed, stating that 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"- e93 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra'; SemiAnalysis said this could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no pro\",\n   \"- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post published Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which is designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107, e125, e126, e167, e172, e176, e182, e184; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its next-generation Kyber rack system is delayed, stating that 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e109, e129, e173, e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e93 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra'; SemiAnalysis said this could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no pro\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e108, e168, e174, e178, e185; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e107 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com (beincrypto.com original by Lockridge Okoth); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis alleges that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 high-density rack architecture, built for Rubin Ultra GPUs, has slipped by more than 12 months, blaming persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Quote: The clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year. … SemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e109 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (beincrypto.com original by Lockridge Okoth); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its product roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e93 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra'; SemiAnalysis said this could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n   \"- e125 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028. The Kyber server cabinets, designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via Nvidia's NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman) reported the claim on 2026-07-06.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e126 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture was delayed to 2028 because the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. It also said Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve a similar power output — was scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its 'odd design and heavy operational burden.'\\n    Quote: However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback. … “Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” SemiAnalysis said, adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e127 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said the manufacturing issues affecting Kyber will also impact Nvidia's planned NVL576 system — which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches — meaning that system will likely be delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis said this leaves Nvidia with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\\n    Quote: The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. … All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e128 [third_party_view; DatacenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] A week before the Kyber report, SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design, also due to 'manufacturing execution concerns.' The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” … The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e169, e175, e187; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e129 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson (reported by DatacenterDynamics); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] In response to a DatacenterDynamics request for comment on the Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e138 [rumour; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across the company's upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems, according to 24/7 Wall St.; the report does not identify the source of the delay reports, name which Vera Rubin products are affected, or give a new timing.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e167 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis (report on X), relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that NVIDIA's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically that the PCB midplane, a central board connecting all components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. The delay report was relayed by the-decoder.com.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems. … The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e168 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's planned alternative design called NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely, after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\\n    Quote: A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e169 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that the more powerful version of NVIDIA's upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e128; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, leaving NVIDIA without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now — a gap that could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e186; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e171 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, relayed by the-decoder.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, NVIDIA plans to make up for the shortfall created by the next-generation system setbacks by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"- e172 [rumour; Investing.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Quote: Su